###Setting up a proportion

Corresponding sides of similar triangles are proportional, so we can set up and solve a proportion:

$\dfrac{DE}{DH}=\dfrac{DF}{DG}$

Side $\overline{DE}$ is equal to $\overline{DH}+\blueE{\overline{EH}}$ and we need to find the length of $\blueE{\overline{EH}}$.

We picked side $\overline{DH}$ because it corresponds to side ${\overline{DE}}$.

Then we picked another pair of corresponding sides whose lengths we already knew.

Side $\overline{DF}$ is equal to $\overline{DG}+{\overline{GF}}$.

$\begin{array}{rcl}
&\triangle DEF
\\
\text{hypotenuse}
&\overbrace{\underbrace{\!\!\!\!\!\left\{ 
\dfrac{DH+\blueE{EH}}{DH}=\dfrac{DG+GF}{DG}
\right\}\!\!\!\!\!}}
&{\text{short} \atop \text{leg}}
\\
&\triangle DHG
\end{array}$
